\f1\b \cf0 Functions.R: 
\f0\b0 This script contains customized functions for accessing, assembling, and wrangling data within the 00_get_data.R script. The `get_daymet()` function is used for downloading and formatting gridded meteorological data from the Daymet gridded, long-term data product, leveraging the `daymetr` package. The `sites_filtered_function()` is used to aid in selecting specific Phenocam sites according to criteria. The `create_raster()` function aids in turning a large tabular dataset of the northern great plains into a raster stack. \
\
\pard\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 \ul \ulc0 Data information:
\f0\b0 \ulnone \
\
The data folder contains multiple subfolders, which are generally contain either single-site or multi-site data. Note that the \'91GADM\'92 folder contains spatial data generate in the process of creating figure 4.\
\
-
\f1\b Site-scale data
\f0\b0  (specific coordinates) are found in folders \'91ap\'92 and \'91sgs\'92. These two folders are derived from the same workflow and contain MODIS-based GPP and NPP data and Daymet-based meteorological data for specific coordinates. These data underlie the plots in figures 1b,c and 2 in the manuscript. An exception is the \'91cperagm_GR_1000_1day.csv\'92 dataset in the \'91sgs\'92 folder, which contains canopy greenness data for a single location (the same location as for the \'91sgs\'92 data), used to generate figure S1.\
\
-
\f1\b Multi-site data
\f0\b0  are found in the \'91phenocam\'92 and \'91ngp\'92 folders. The \'91phenocam\'92 folder contains daily greenness chromatic coordinate (GCC) data for select deciduous broadleaf or grassland sites in North America. The \'91ngp\'92 folder contains large datasets (eventually converted into a raster; ngp_raster_stack.tif) for the entire Northern Great Plains Ecoregion of North America. These data were generated from another project, and contain summaries of GPP (seasonal) and NPP (annual) anomalies (from their mean) during the most extreme (lowest annual precipitation) droughts in the MODIS record. These data can be viewed as a large-scale version of the site-specific data in the \'91sgs\'92 and \'91ap\'92 folders.\
}